    answer: '<p>Home environments lack the built-in focus cues that offices provide:</p><ul><li><strong>No social accountability:</strong> No one sees you scrolling Instagram</li><li><strong>Comfort equals distraction:</strong> Your couch, TV, and fridge are steps away</li><li><strong>Context confusion:</strong> Your brain associates home with relaxation, not work</li><li><strong>Unlimited connectivity:</strong> More devices, more temptation</li></ul><p>The solution is creating artificial structure and environmental cues that signal "work mode" to your brain.</p>'
  },
  {
    question: 'How long can the brain stay focused?',
    answer: '<p>Focus isn\'t infinite—our brains need breaks:</p><ul><li><strong>Sustained attention:</strong> 25-90 minutes maximum for most people</li><li><strong>Ultradian rhythms:</strong> Natural 90-minute cycles of alertness</li><li><strong>Attention residue:</strong> After each task switch, focus degrades for 15-25 minutes</li></ul><p>Plan work in focused sprints (45-90 min) with genuine breaks. Working 8 hours without breaks doesn\'t mean 8 hours of focus—it means declining effectiveness.</p>'
  },
  {
    question: 'Should I listen to music while working?',
    answer: '<p>It depends on the task and your personality:</p><ul><li><strong>Repetitive tasks:</strong> Music can boost mood and productivity</li><li><strong>Complex tasks:</strong> Silence or ambient sounds usually work better</li><li><strong>Lyrics:</strong> Generally distracting for tasks involving language (writing, reading)</li><li><strong>Familiar music:</strong> Less distracting than new music since your brain doesn\'t "listen"</li></ul><p>Experiment with different sounds for different work types. Many find lo-fi, classical, or nature sounds optimal.</p>'
  },
  {
    question: 'How do I stay focused with kids at home?',
    answer: '<p>Working with children requires structure and communication:</p><ul><li><strong>Visual signals:</strong> "Green light/red light" system when you can be interrupted</li><li><strong>Activity boxes:</strong> Special activities only available during your focus time</li><li><strong>Shift work:</strong> If possible, trade focus time with a partner</li><li><strong>Scheduled check-ins:</strong> Set specific times for kid questions</li><li><strong>Realistic expectations:</strong> Shorter focus blocks may be necessary</li></ul><p>Some parents find early mornings (before kids wake) or late evenings most productive.</p>'
  },
  {
    question: 'What\'s the best time of day to focus?',
    answer: '<p>Peak focus times vary by chronotype:</p><ul><li><strong>Early birds:</strong> Best focus 8am-12pm, energy drops afternoon</li><li><strong>Night owls:</strong> Focus improves throughout day, peaks evening</li><li><strong>Most people:</strong> Focus peak mid-morning, slump after lunch, second peak late afternoon</li></ul><p>Track your focus quality for a week. Schedule deep work during your peak hours and meetings/admin during lower-energy periods.</p>'
  },
  {
    question: 'How do I stop checking my phone constantly?',
    answer: '<p>Phone addiction is designed by experts—fight back with systems:</p><ul><li><strong>Physical separation:</strong> Phone in another room during focus time</li><li><strong>Grayscale mode:</strong> Makes the phone less visually appealing</li><li><strong>App limits:</strong> Use Screen Time (iOS) or Digital Wellbeing (Android)</li><li><strong>Delete trigger apps:</strong> Remove social media apps, use browser versions only</li><li><strong>Scheduled checks:</strong> Check phone at breaks only, not continuously</li></ul><p>The goal isn\'t zero phone use—it\'s intentional use on your schedule.</p>'
  },
  {
    question: 'Does caffeine help or hurt focus?',
    answer: '<p>Caffeine can enhance focus when used strategically:</p><ul><li><strong>Optimal timing:</strong> 90-120 minutes after waking (cortisol is naturally high earlier)</li><li><strong>Moderate dose:</strong> 100-200mg (1-2 cups coffee) for focus, more can cause anxiety</li><li><strong>Cut-off time:</strong> No caffeine after 2pm to protect sleep quality</li><li><strong>Tolerance:</strong> Regular use reduces effectiveness; occasional use or cycling works better</li></ul><p>Good sleep is more important than caffeine. Address sleep issues before relying on stimulants.</p>'
  },
  {
    question: 'How do I refocus after being interrupted?',
    answer: '<p>Interruption recovery is a skill you can develop:</p><ul><li><strong>Document your place:</strong> Before handling interruption, write where you were</li><li><strong>Set a return timer:</strong> "I\'ll handle this and return in 5 minutes"</li><li><strong>Context reload:</strong> Reread last paragraph/code block before continuing</li><li><strong>Mini-ritual:</strong> Brief action (deep breath, sip of water) to reset</li></ul><p>Studies show it takes 23 minutes to fully recover focus after an interruption. Protecting focus time prevents the recovery cost.</p>'
  }
];

