  <h2>Conclusion: Focus Is a Skill</h2>
  <p>
    Here's the truth: <strong>focus is not a fixed trait</strong>. It's a skill that strengthens with
    practice and weakens with neglect.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every time you resist a distraction, you build focus capacity. Every time you give in, you reinforce
    the distraction habit. The good news: small changes compound.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Your action plan:</strong>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Today:</strong> Do a notification audit—disable everything non-essential</li>
    <li><strong>This week:</strong> Set up a dedicated workspace and try blocking distracting sites</li>
    <li><strong>This month:</strong> Develop a focus ritual and establish communication boundaries with household</li>
    <li><strong>Ongoing:</strong> Protect your peak focus hours for deep work</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    You don't need perfect focus—you need <strong>protected focus</strong>. Create the conditions where
    focus is the default, not the exception, and watch your productivity transform.
  </p>

